      "status": "ok",
      "style": "plain",
      "text": "The sky looks blue because the atmosphere scatters blue light more strongly than red light. Sunlight contains all colours. The atmosphere scatters short wavelengths more strongly than long wavelengths.\n\nBecause blue light has a short wavelength, it is scattered across the sky. This scattering is called Rayleigh scattering.\n\nHowever, light travels through more atmosphere at sunset. Blue light is scattered away and leaves reds and oranges. In short, this is why the sky looks blue during the day and red at sunset."
